Finally, to further verify whether the identified disease factors could capture clinical heterogeneity in OCD,we performed affinity propagation clustering to uncover putative subtypes of OCD from factor compositions (Frey and Dueck 2007).

## Results

## Demographics and clinical characteristics

The demographics and clinical characteristics of subjects were included in Table 1. These was no significant differences between patients with OCD and HCs in terms of sex and age. However, patients with OCD exhibited lower educational level than HCs.

## Four robust disease factors were identified using our framework

The individualized GMV differences were divided into 4 (2 positive and 2 negative) disease factors according to the stability values (Fig. S1). The distributions of 4 disease factors were showed in Fig. 2A (the top 5% voxels of each disease factor were displayed just for show) and the corresponding factor compositions for each patient was drawn in Fig. 2B. Positive factor 1 was mainly associated with the ventromedial prefrontal cortex (vmPFC), striatum, insula, bilateral middle temporal gyrus, and middle occipital gyrus. Positive factor 2 was mainly associated with the bilateral thalamus, putamen, middle temporal gyrus, and postcentral gyrus. Negative factor 1 was mainly associated with right putamen, hippocampus, and right inferior temporal gyrus. Negative factor 2 was mainly associated with vmPFC, insula, middle cingulum, and bilateral middle temporal gyrus.

